### What is a Kafka topic?

A topic is purely a logical name that categorizes a stream of events. It does not store anything by itself. When you create a topic you specify how many partitions it has, because partitions are where the real data lives. In a banking system you might have topics named `transfers`, `transactions`, and `trade-settled`, each representing a distinct event category.

```java
package com.bank.admin;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.admin.AdminClient;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.admin.NewTopic;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Properties;

public class TopicCreator {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "localhost:9092");

        try (AdminClient admin = AdminClient.create(props)) {
            NewTopic transfersTopic = new NewTopic("transfers", 6, (short) 3);
            admin.createTopics(Collections.singleton(transfersTopic)).all().get();
            System.out.println("Topic 'transfers' created: 6 partitions, RF=3");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Topic 'transfers' created: 6 partitions, RF=3
```

### What is a Kafka partition?

A partition is an ordered, append-only sequence of records. New records are always appended to the end, and records are never modified once written. Kafka guarantees ordering only within a single partition, not across partitions. Each partition is stored as a set of segment files on a broker's disk, and it can be replicated to other brokers for fault tolerance. The number of partitions determines the maximum parallelism of a consumer group, because one partition is consumed by exactly one consumer in a group at a time.

```java
package com.bank.producer;

import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferProducer {

    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate;

    public TransferProducer(KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate) {
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
    }

    public void publishTransfer(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        String event = "{\"accountId\":\"" + accountId
            + "\",\"amount\":" + amount + "}";
        // Key = accountId ensures all transfers for SAV2001
        // go to the SAME partition and stay ordered
        kafkaTemplate.send("transfers", accountId, event);
        System.out.println("Published transfer for " + accountId
            + " (partition chosen by key hash)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Published transfer for SAV2001 (partition chosen by key hash)
-> All SAV2001 events land in partition 2 (determined by hash)
-> All SAV3002 events land in partition 5 (different hash)
```

### What is a Kafka offset?

An offset is a 64-bit integer assigned by the broker when a record is appended. Offsets start at 0 and increase by one for each new record in that partition. Offsets are per-partition, so partition 0 and partition 1 each have their own offset 0. A consumer reads records in offset order within a partition and periodically commits the offset of the last processed record. The committed offset is stored in the internal `__consumer_offsets` topic.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecord;
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class TransferConsumer {

    @KafkaListener(topics = "transfers", groupId = "settlement-group")
    public void processTransfer(ConsumerRecord<String, String> record) {
        System.out.println("Received transfer:"
            + " topic=" + record.topic()
            + " partition=" + record.partition()
            + " offset=" + record.offset()
            + " key=" + record.key()
            + " value=" + record.value());
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Received transfer: topic=transfers partition=2 offset=0 key=SAV2001 value={...}
Received transfer: topic=transfers partition=2 offset=1 key=SAV2001 value={...}
Received transfer: topic=transfers partition=5 offset=0 key=SAV3002 value={...}
```

### How does the message key determine the partition?

If you produce with a key, Kafka hashes the key and uses the hash to pick the partition, so the same key always lands in the same partition. For banking, keying by account id means all events for one account are ordered in the same partition. If you produce without a key, Kafka distributes records across partitions in a round-robin or sticky fashion for balanced load.

```java
package com.bank.partitioning;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.internals.DefaultPartitioner;
import org.apache.kafka.common.utils.Utils;

public class PartitionCalculator {

    public static int calculatePartition(String key, int numPartitions) {
        // Kafka's default: murmur2 hash of key, mod number of partitions
        int hash = Utils.toPositive(Utils.murmur2(key.getBytes()));
        int partition = hash % numPartitions;
        return partition;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int partitions = 6;
        System.out.println("SAV2001 -> partition "
            + calculatePartition("SAV2001", partitions));
        System.out.println("SAV3002 -> partition "
            + calculatePartition("SAV3002", partitions));
        System.out.println("SAV2001 -> partition "
            + calculatePartition("SAV2001", partitions)
            + " (same key = same partition, always)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
SAV2001 -> partition 2
SAV3002 -> partition 5
SAV2001 -> partition 2 (same key = same partition, always)
```

### How do offsets enable replay?

When a consumer restarts, it resumes from the last committed offset. A consumer can also seek to an older offset to replay data. This is how the fraud team at National Bank of India re-reads last week's events to retrain a detection model without disturbing other consumer groups.

```java
package com.bank.replay;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.KafkaConsumer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecords;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecord;
import org.apache.kafka.common.TopicPartition;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Properties;

public class OffsetReplayExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "localhost:9092");
        props.put("group.id", "fraud-replay-group");
        props.put("key.deserializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        props.put("value.deserializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");

        KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(props);
        TopicPartition tp = new TopicPartition("transfers", 2);
        consumer.assign(Collections.singleton(tp));

        // Seek to offset 100 to replay from that point
        consumer.seek(tp, 100);
        System.out.println("Seeking to offset 100 on partition 2");

        ConsumerRecords<String, String> records =
            consumer.poll(Duration.ofSeconds(5));
        for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> record : records) {
            System.out.println("Replayed: offset=" + record.offset()
                + " key=" + record.key());
        }
        consumer.close();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Seeking to offset 100 on partition 2
Replayed: offset=100 key=SAV2001
Replayed: offset=101 key=SAV2001
Replayed: offset=102 key=SAV2001
```

### How is a topic different from a partition? (comparison table)

A topic is logical; a partition is physical. A topic is a name; a partition stores the actual data.

| Feature | Topic | Partition |
|---|---|---|
| Nature | Logical name (category) | Physical log (data storage) |
| Stores data | No (just a grouping) | Yes (append-only log on disk) |
| Ordering | No ordering guarantee | Strict ordering within partition |
| Number | One per event category | Many per topic (configurable) |
| Parallelism | N/A | One partition = one consumer max |
| Replication | N/A | Each partition replicated to N brokers |

```java
package com.bank.comparison;

public class TopicVsPartition {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Topic 'transfers':");
        System.out.println("  Logical name, no data storage itself");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Partitions of 'transfers' (6 total):");
        System.out.println("  Partition 0: [offset 0][offset 1][offset 2]...");
        System.out.println("  Partition 1: [offset 0][offset 1]...");
        System.out.println("  Partition 2: [offset 0][offset 1][offset 2][offset 3]...");
        System.out.println("  Each partition is an independent ordered log");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Topic 'transfers':
  Logical name, no data storage itself

Partitions of 'transfers' (6 total):
  Partition 0: [offset 0][offset 1][offset 2]...
  Partition 1: [offset 0][offset 1]...
  Partition 2: [offset 0][offset 1][offset 2][offset 3]...
  Each partition is an independent ordered log
```

### What are the edge cases with topics, partitions, and offsets?

One edge case is producing without a key. Without a key, Kafka uses a sticky partitioner that sends batches to random partitions, so you lose per-entity ordering. Another edge case is increasing partitions later: it changes the key-to-partition mapping, so a key that was on partition 2 may now land on partition 4, breaking ordering for existing keys.

```java
package com.bank.edge;

import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;

public class NoKeyProducer {

    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate;

    public NoKeyProducer(KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate) {
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
    }

    public void publishWithoutKey(String event) {
        // No key: partition is chosen by sticky partitioner (round-robin batches)
        // SAV2001 events may land on ANY partition, breaking ordering
        kafkaTemplate.send("transfers", event);
        System.out.println("Published without key (no ordering guarantee)");
    }

    public void publishWithKey(String accountId, String event) {
        // With key: hash(accountId) determines partition
        // SAV2001 events always land on the SAME partition
        kafkaTemplate.send("transfers", accountId, event);
        System.out.println("Published with key " + accountId
            + " (ordering guaranteed per key)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Published without key (no ordering guarantee)
-> Event could land on partition 0, 3, 5 (random)

Published with key SAV2001 (ordering guaranteed per key)
-> Event always lands on partition 2 (hash of SAV2001)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Offsets are the key to Kafka's replay capability and its delivery guarantees. Committing the offset after processing gives at-least-once delivery, while committing before processing gives at-most-once. Because offsets are durable and per-partition, a brand-new consumer group can read the entire history of a topic from offset 0 while another group reads only new records from the latest offset, both at the same time without interference.

```java
package com.bank.senior;

public class OffsetSemantics {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Offset Commit Timing and Delivery Semantics:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("At-most-once:  commit offset BEFORE processing");
        System.out.println("  Risk: crash after commit, message lost");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("At-least-once: commit offset AFTER processing");
        System.out.println("  Risk: crash after processing, message redelivered");
        System.out.println("  Solution: idempotent consumer");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("auto.offset.reset:");
        System.out.println("  earliest -> new group reads ALL retained records");
        System.out.println("  latest   -> new group reads only NEW records");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Offset Commit Timing and Delivery Semantics:

At-most-once:  commit offset BEFORE processing
  Risk: crash after commit, message lost

At-least-once: commit offset AFTER processing
  Risk: crash after processing, message redelivered
  Solution: idempotent consumer

auto.offset.reset:
  earliest -> new group reads ALL retained records
  latest   -> new group reads only NEW records
```
